00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:38.190
All right.

00:02:38.190 --> 00:02:40.860
Now, let's dig in to
understand the core components

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:44.130
of Windows 365 for Agents.

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:44.730
All right.

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:46.320
What is Windows 365 for Agents?

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:48.300
At its core, it's three things.

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:51.630
First, it's cloud PCs
built for agents.

00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:53.400
These are full
Windows environments,

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:56.850
the same Windows 365 Cloud
PCs that people use today

00:02:56.850 --> 00:03:00.210
but provisioned, connected,
and managed for agent use.

00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:02.790
Your agent has a
desktop, a screen,

00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:04.680
all the peripherals it needs,

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:08.040
and can access any application
that runs on Windows.

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:11.430
Second, it's an MCP tool layer.

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:14.250
MCP, the Model Context Protocol,
which is the open standard

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:16.320
for connecting agents to tools.

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:18.420
We ship a set of MCP tools

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:21.450
that lets your agent provision
a cloud PC, connect to it,

00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:24.540
interact with it, and
manage its lifecycle.

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:27.150
If your agent
framework speaks MCP,

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:29.940
it works with Windows
365 for Agents.

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:33.330
Third, it is enterprise
control and operations

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:34.830
that you know already.

00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:37.050
Cloud PC pools are
managed in Intune.

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:40.170
Identity goes through
intra-agentic identity.

00:03:40.170 --> 00:03:41.370
Policies, compliance,

00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:43.740
conditional access,
it all just works.

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:45.960
There's nothing new
for IT to learn.

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:48.720
They manage agent
cloud PCs the same way

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:51.360
that they manage user cloud PCs.

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:54.180
And all of this sits
within Agent 365.

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:58.110
The MCP tool is managed
within the Agent 365 portal,

00:03:58.110 --> 00:04:00.570
and all of the observability
and governance

00:04:00.570 --> 00:04:04.620
for your Windows 365-enabled
agents surface the same way

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:07.800
that they do for any
Agent 365-enabled agent.

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:11.970
This makes it easy for your AI
admins to configure Windows 365

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:14.880
for Agents alongside
all the other agents

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:17.010
in your enterprise tenant.

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:17.730
All right.

00:04:17.730 --> 00:04:20.700
Now that we have an
idea of the platform,

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:24.120
let me invite my good
friend Jiachen from Simular

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:26.940
to show you a
real-world use case.

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:27.810
JIACHEN YANG: Hello, everyone.

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:29.880
I'm Jiachen Yang at Simular.

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:32.850
Our mission is to solve
digital autonomy and use it

00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:35.010
to improve everyone's lives.

00:04:35.010 --> 00:04:37.830
Since becoming a Lighthouse
customer in November,

00:04:37.830 --> 00:04:41.370
we've partnered with the Windows
team to run our AI agent product

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:45.480
on Windows 365 for Agents
so they're enterprise ready

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:48.380
with security and IT governance.

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:50.120
Let me introduce you to Sai,

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:53.660
Simular's always-available
agentic coworker.

00:04:53.660 --> 00:04:56.300
Sai sees the screen,
moves the mouse,

00:04:56.300 --> 00:04:59.120
and types on the keyboard,
just like a human.

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:03.290
It also uses APIs, operates
the terminal, and writes code.

00:05:03.290 --> 00:05:05.870
This means it works with
your existing software

00:05:05.870 --> 00:05:10.370
out of the box, including legacy
enterprise tools with no APIs,

00:05:10.370 --> 00:05:14.160
most of which are running on
Windows and Windows only.

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:18.120
That's why Windows 365 for
Agents is such a natural fit

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:23.160
since Sai can operate anything
that runs on a Windows desktop.

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:27.120
As more people rely on Sai for
repeatable and recurring tasks,

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:29.790
we need to scale up
and down flexibly.

00:05:29.790 --> 00:05:35.160
With Windows 365 for Agents, Sai
can spin up cloud PCs on demand,

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:38.340
scale up to maximize
performance on large workloads,

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:40.860
and spin them down after
finishing the work

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:43.080
to minimize cost.

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:45.150
Additionally, Windows 365

00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:48.660
for Agents gives Sai a
secure execution layer,

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:52.860
isolated cloud PCs governed
by Entra ID and Intune

00:05:52.860 --> 00:05:55.530
so developers can
focus on agent logic

00:05:55.530 --> 00:05:58.860
and not enterprise requirements.

00:05:58.860 --> 00:06:01.680
Let's look at an example.

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:04.920
Here, Sai runs an overnight
claims processing workflow

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:08.460
in a Contoso claims
app with no APIs.

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:11.790
Users can launch Sai using
a single instruction.

00:06:11.790 --> 00:06:16.290
Sai opens and reads the scanned
handwritten claim form.

00:06:16.290 --> 00:06:19.770
It extracts key fields
such as claimant, policy,

00:06:19.770 --> 00:06:21.900
and estimated damages.

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:26.250
It directly enters it into
the application with no API,

00:06:26.250 --> 00:06:28.440
just the user interface.

00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:31.080
It fills out the policy
details such as number,

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:34.590
coverage type, and deductible.

00:06:34.590 --> 00:06:38.520
Notice how the zip code has
some duplicate numbers.

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:43.380
Sai is able to catch that and
fix the data entry error.

00:06:43.380 --> 00:06:46.890
It keeps going, selecting "Burst
Pipe" from the dropdown menu,

00:06:46.890 --> 00:06:50.970
navigating the UI
just like a human.

00:06:50.970 --> 00:06:53.310
While it's doing this,
Sai can alert you

00:06:53.310 --> 00:06:56.070
to its current progress
and always allow you

00:06:56.070 --> 00:06:59.460
to take control when needed.

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:03.630
The claim form is filled, and
Sai stands by for the next one.

00:07:03.630 --> 00:07:06.990
All this can be done on
Windows 365 for Agents.

00:07:06.990 --> 00:07:10.380
The bottom line is that
Simular and Sai is the brain

00:07:10.380 --> 00:07:13.500
for an autonomous
computer, and Windows 365

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:17.460
for Agents provides the
secure execution environment.

00:07:17.460 --> 00:07:20.250
Now let me hand over to Sam.

00:07:20.250 --> 00:07:21.570
SAM SHAPIRO: So that's
what's possible.

00:07:21.570 --> 00:07:25.350
Real agents, real enterprise
workloads running today.

00:07:25.350 --> 00:07:27.660
Now let's take a look at
the experiences we've built

00:07:27.660 --> 00:07:30.600
within the Windows 365
for Agents platform.

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:32.730
I'm going to walk you through
how agent builders can integrate

00:07:32.730 --> 00:07:36.480
the Windows 365 for Agents
MCP server, configure agents

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:39.270
