00:07:39.270 --> 00:07:44.250
with Windows 365 for Agents,
enable cloud-using agents.

00:07:44.250 --> 00:07:46.470
Let me start out by
demonstrating how easy it is

00:07:46.470 --> 00:07:48.810
to integrate and test
within the Windows 365

00:07:48.810 --> 00:07:51.210
for Agents MCP server.

00:07:51.210 --> 00:07:54.630
What you're looking at here is a
sample computer use agent built

00:07:54.630 --> 00:07:59.040
on Azure OpenAI and powered
by a GPT 5.4 model.

00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:01.140
The logic for this
agent is pretty simple.

00:08:01.140 --> 00:08:03.960
It receives a user message,
connects to the MCP server

00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:07.620
to get its toolkit, provisions
and controls a cloud PC session,

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:09.390
and then enters its core loop.

00:08:09.390 --> 00:08:12.690
Capture a screenshot, reason
over it, take an action,

00:08:12.690 --> 00:08:15.240
and repeat until the
task is complete.

00:08:15.240 --> 00:08:18.390
It's a simple agent, but it's
fully integrated with Agent 365

00:08:18.390 --> 00:08:21.220
and Windows 365 for Agents.

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:25.830
The agent is made up
of a few key pieces.

00:08:25.830 --> 00:08:28.830
The most important one, the
computer use orchestrator,

00:08:28.830 --> 00:08:31.770
this is where the screenshot
action repeat loop lives,

00:08:31.770 --> 00:08:33.120
along with a clear prompt

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:36.190
that tells the agent what
it's supposed to be doing.

00:08:39.270 --> 00:08:42.090
Inside myagent.cs, you'll
find all the integration

00:08:42.090 --> 00:08:45.000
with Agent 365, the
observability tooling,

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:47.820
and the Windows 365
computer use logic.

00:08:47.820 --> 00:08:51.300
At runtime, based on the tooling
configuration that we specify,

00:08:51.300 --> 00:08:54.660
the agent reaches out, discovers
what MCP server it has access

00:08:54.660 --> 00:08:57.990
to, and based on the model
context protocol metadata,

00:08:57.990 --> 00:09:00.700
knows how and when to call them.

00:09:09.330 --> 00:09:11.160
And this is the
tooling manifest.

00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:14.820
It's where we declare which MCP
server this agent has access to.

00:09:14.820 --> 00:09:16.260
Right now, it's empty.

00:09:16.260 --> 00:09:17.790
No server is configured,

00:09:17.790 --> 00:09:21.300
so the agent can't actually
access Windows 365 for Agents.

00:09:21.300 --> 00:09:22.680
To see what's available
in the tenant,

00:09:22.680 --> 00:09:27.330
I'll use the Agent 365 CLI
and run list available.

00:09:27.330 --> 00:09:30.150
You can see there's a rich set
of MCP servers ready to go

00:09:30.150 --> 00:09:33.060
out of the box available
through Agent 365.

00:09:33.060 --> 00:09:36.390
The one we care about today is
the Windows 365 computer use

00:09:36.390 --> 00:09:37.920
MCP server.

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:41.010
Wiring it up is genuinely
a configuration change,

00:09:41.010 --> 00:09:42.510
not a code rewrite.

00:09:42.510 --> 00:09:47.070
I just run add MCP server
through the A365 CLI tool,

00:09:47.070 --> 00:09:49.890
and it pulls the MCP
server into the manifest

00:09:49.890 --> 00:09:52.020
and writes all the
configuration the agent needs

00:09:52.020 --> 00:09:54.600
to authorize and use it.

00:09:54.600 --> 00:09:55.860
And there it is.

00:09:55.860 --> 00:09:58.800
The manifest is updated, and
this agent now has access

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:01.440
to every tool inside
of that MCP server.

00:10:01.440 --> 00:10:03.450
While we're here,
let's add one more.

00:10:03.450 --> 00:10:06.990
How about the mail tools so
our agent can access Outlook?

00:10:06.990 --> 00:10:10.920
Same command, same experience.

00:10:10.920 --> 00:10:13.770
Done. The agent now has a
much more powerful toolkit

00:10:13.770 --> 00:10:15.420
with almost no effort.

00:10:15.420 --> 00:10:17.040
Now let's run it locally

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:19.680
and make sure the MCP
server is actually working.

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:21.600
Kicking off, the
local build spins

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:24.280
up the agent as a local server.

00:10:29.840 --> 00:10:32.990
From there, I can open
the Agent 365 playground

00:10:32.990 --> 00:10:36.390
and connect directly
to the local agent.

00:10:38.670 --> 00:10:40.740
You can see I'm now
connected to the agent.

00:10:40.740 --> 00:10:44.400
It has access to the Windows
365 for Agents MCP server,

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:46.590
and I've got live
logs on the right.

00:10:46.590 --> 00:10:48.510
This is a really tight
development loop.

00:10:48.510 --> 00:10:51.030
I can see exactly how the
agent is using each tool,

00:10:51.030 --> 00:10:52.590
where I can make
it more efficient

00:10:52.590 --> 00:10:54.210
and optimize in real time.

00:10:54.210 --> 00:10:55.740
It's a great environment
for iterating

00:10:55.740 --> 00:10:58.090
on your agent and
your tool calls.

00:10:59.940 --> 00:11:02.190
For our first test, I'll
ask it to open Notepad

00:11:02.190 --> 00:11:03.840
and type a simple phrase.

00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:05.550
You can see it step
through the flow.

00:11:05.550 --> 00:11:08.670
First, it acquires a Windows
365 Cloud PC session,

00:11:08.670 --> 00:11:10.710
checking which pools
it has access to

00:11:10.710 --> 00:11:13.680
and which machines are available
to check out right now.

00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:16.290
The first-time spin-up
takes a few moments.

00:11:16.290 --> 00:11:17.220
And there we go.

00:11:17.220 --> 00:11:18.660
The session is starting.

00:11:18.660 --> 00:11:19.770
We've configured the agent

00:11:19.770 --> 00:11:21.960
to generate a screenshot
on every step.

00:11:21.960 --> 00:11:24.120
So as it passes the
commands to the Cloud PC

00:11:24.120 --> 00:11:27.820
and performs actions, each one
is captured along the way.

00:11:35.880 --> 00:11:37.410
Task completed successfully.

00:11:37.410 --> 00:11:40.690
Let's look at the screenshots
to see what actually happened.

00:11:41.760 --> 00:11:43.740
Four screenshots in total.

00:11:43.740 --> 00:11:46.320
And in the final screenshot
we can see the Cloud PC

00:11:46.320 --> 00:11:48.240
with a Notepad
window open reading,

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:50.550
this is a demo of a Windows 365.

00:11:50.550 --> 00:11:52.830
Exactly what we asked for.

00:11:52.830 --> 00:11:55.650
Now let's jump into how
agents that are integrated

00:11:55.650 --> 00:11:59.970
with Agent 365 and Windows 365
for Agents can be made available

00:11:59.970 --> 00:12:02.650
to your entire
enterprise tenant.

00:12:03.810 --> 00:12:06.000
I'm going to walk you through
how an admin configures a

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:09.540
Windows 365 for Agents
blueprint in their tenant.

00:12:09.540 --> 00:12:13.860
This is Agent 365 inside of
the Microsoft Admin Center.

00:12:13.860 --> 00:12:15.480
It gives you an overarching view

00:12:15.480 --> 00:12:18.640
of every agent in
your organization.

00:12:19.950 --> 00:12:25.200
If I select a specific blueprint
in my tenant, I can see all

00:12:25.200 --> 00:12:27.480
of the details of
that blueprint.

00:12:27.480 --> 00:12:28.560
I can see it has access

00:12:28.560 --> 00:12:31.800
to the Windows 365
computer use MCP server,

00:12:31.800 --> 00:12:34.750
as well as a few
other MCP servers.

00:12:38.250 --> 00:12:39.990
Right now, no users
are permitted

00:12:39.990 --> 00:12:41.370
to create instances of it.

00:12:41.370 --> 00:12:43.380
So let me update the
permission so that every user

00:12:43.380 --> 00:12:45.030
in my tenant can
spin up an instance,

00:12:45.030 --> 00:12:47.220
effectively giving my
entire enterprise access

00:12:47.220 --> 00:12:50.460
to Windows 365 for Agents.

00:12:50.460 --> 00:12:53.010
I'll also confirm that the
server itself is broadly

00:12:53.010 --> 00:12:55.680
available by scrolling
through the tools list

00:12:55.680 --> 00:12:57.420
and finding the Windows 365

00:12:57.420 --> 00:13:01.710
for Agents MCP server
available through Agent 365.

00:13:01.710 --> 00:13:05.040
Let's update the status
here to unblocked.

00:13:05.040 --> 00:13:10.770
Now my agent can access Windows
365 for Agents MCP server.

00:13:10.770 --> 00:13:14.380
Pool creation and assignment
happen in Intune.

00:13:15.570 --> 00:13:18.960
Inside of the Intune Admin
Center, under "Devices"

00:13:18.960 --> 00:13:22.680
in Windows 365 Cloud PCs,
there's a dedicated experience

00:13:22.680 --> 00:13:26.260
for provisioning cloud PCs
specifically for agents.

00:13:27.510 --> 00:13:30.690
I'll create a new
provisioning policy.

00:13:30.690 --> 00:13:33.090
To get started, I'll add a
name to my provisioning policy

00:13:33.090 --> 00:13:34.110
so that I can identify it

00:13:34.110 --> 00:13:36.810
if I have multiple policies
created in my tenant.

00:13:36.810 --> 00:13:38.460
Next, I add a brief description

00:13:38.460 --> 00:13:40.560
to help identify what
provisioning policy will be

00:13:40.560 --> 00:13:41.370
used for.

00:13:41.370 --> 00:13:43.080
I select a billing plan
which will be used

00:13:43.080 --> 00:13:45.630
for consumption-based charge
and then associate a number

00:13:45.630 --> 00:13:48.220
of always available cloud PCs.

00:13:52.020 --> 00:13:54.240
Next, I add my agents.

00:13:54.240 --> 00:13:56.730
This is actually where the
agent instances get assigned

00:13:56.730 --> 00:13:57.870
to the cloud PC.

00:13:57.870 --> 00:14:00.990
I won't add any now, but as
agent instances get created

00:14:00.990 --> 00:14:03.030
by employees, I'll have
the ability to add them

00:14:03.030 --> 00:14:06.390
to my existing
provisioning policies.

00:14:06.390 --> 00:14:07.980
Next, I have an image.

00:14:07.980 --> 00:14:09.450
Out of the box, custom images

00:14:09.450 --> 00:14:11.310
and gallery images
are supported.

00:14:11.310 --> 00:14:14.100
Today I'll be using
a gallery image.

00:14:14.100 --> 00:14:18.120
Finally, I can select a language
for this provisioning policy.

00:14:18.120 --> 00:14:19.740
Here's all the
information associated

00:14:19.740 --> 00:14:21.810
with my provisioning
policy, and I can create it

00:14:21.810 --> 00:14:24.270
by clicking the "Create" button.

00:14:24.270 --> 00:14:26.550
Now that we've enabled the
agent blueprint in the tenant

00:14:26.550 --> 00:14:29.040
and created a provisioning
policy, let's take a look

00:14:29.040 --> 00:14:31.950
at how enterprise workers
can create agent instances,

00:14:31.950 --> 00:14:35.280
get them assigned a provisioning
policy, and perform real work

00:14:35.280 --> 00:14:38.490
with Windows 365 for Agents.

00:14:38.490 --> 00:14:40.290
To start, I'm in Teams.

00:14:40.290 --> 00:14:41.550
I go to the agent section

00:14:41.550 --> 00:14:43.740
where I can see all
my agents available

00:14:43.740 --> 00:14:45.450
within my enterprise tenant.

00:14:45.450 --> 00:14:46.380
I'll search for the agent

00:14:46.380 --> 00:14:48.390
that I just configured
the blueprint for,

00:14:48.390 --> 00:14:52.470
the Build Win365A Blueprint.

00:14:52.470 --> 00:14:55.800
I can create agent instances
directly with Teams.

00:14:55.800 --> 00:14:58.890
I'll add a name, which will
be used to identify my agent,

00:14:58.890 --> 00:15:00.570
and then I'll add an alias.

00:15:00.570 --> 00:15:03.730
This is how the agent will
be registered in Entra.

00:15:04.860 --> 00:15:07.740
I hit "Create" and now I
can see that my agent is

00:15:07.740 --> 00:15:10.740
in the process of being created.

00:15:10.740 --> 00:15:13.890
During this time, it's creating
an agent instance in Entra,

00:15:13.890 --> 00:15:16.260
pulling down the
blueprint, copying it,

00:15:16.260 --> 00:15:18.270
and assigning the right
permissions that it needs

00:15:18.270 --> 00:15:20.950
to do meaningful
work right away.

00:15:23.700 --> 00:15:25.410
And there's my
agent ready to go.

00:15:25.410 --> 00:15:27.570
I can now chat directly with it.

00:15:27.570 --> 00:15:30.090
Before I can run computer-using
workflows, I need to take

00:15:30.090 --> 00:15:31.260
that agent and give it access

00:15:31.260 --> 00:15:33.930
to a provisioning
policy that I created.

00:15:33.930 --> 00:15:36.420
So now let's imagine
I'm an IT admin asked

00:15:36.420 --> 00:15:39.390
to provide Cloud PC access
to a newly created agent.

00:15:39.390 --> 00:15:41.730
I navigate to provisioning
policies for agents.

00:15:41.730 --> 00:15:46.050
I find the policy that I want
to assign this agent to.

00:15:46.050 --> 00:15:48.960
Here I can see all of the
agents that already have access

00:15:48.960 --> 00:15:50.580
to this Cloud PC pool.

00:15:50.580 --> 00:15:55.350
I'll add my newly created
agent by clicking "Add Agent."

00:15:55.350 --> 00:15:57.930
I see a list of all the
agents in my tenant

00:15:57.930 --> 00:16:00.570
and I select the
relevant agent instance.

00:16:00.570 --> 00:16:03.750
I confirm the assignment,
update the policy,

00:16:03.750 --> 00:16:05.730
and I see a success
message indicating

00:16:05.730 --> 00:16:10.320
that the agent now has
access to the Cloud PC pool.

00:16:10.320 --> 00:16:12.240
So now that the
agent has access,

00:16:12.240 --> 00:16:14.430
let's run a real workflow.

00:16:14.430 --> 00:16:16.080
I'll start with
something simple.

00:16:16.080 --> 00:16:19.680
I ask the agent to open Notepad
and type "This is a build demo."

00:16:19.680 --> 00:16:21.270
It's a basic task
but a great way

00:16:21.270 --> 00:16:23.490
to demonstrate how a
computer-using agent can check

00:16:23.490 --> 00:16:25.860
out a Cloud PC,
open applications,

00:16:25.860 --> 00:16:29.730
and perform real work by typing
and interacting with the system.

00:16:29.730 --> 00:16:33.030
The agent connects to the
Cloud PC, starts a session,

00:16:33.030 --> 00:16:36.270
and provides a folder where
screenshots will be stored.

00:16:36.270 --> 00:16:38.010
The task completes successfully.

00:16:38.010 --> 00:16:40.200
Let's take a look
at what it did.

00:16:40.200 --> 00:16:41.490
In the first step, we see

00:16:41.490 --> 00:16:44.310
that it successfully
checked out a Cloud PC.

00:16:44.310 --> 00:16:47.550
Next, it opened Notepad
and finally it typed

00:16:47.550 --> 00:16:50.340
"This is a build demo"
exactly as requested,

00:16:50.340 --> 00:16:53.700
interacting with the UI
just like a human would.

00:16:53.700 --> 00:16:55.530
Now let's try something
more advanced.

00:16:55.530 --> 00:16:57.060
I'll ask the agent to open Edge,

00:16:57.060 --> 00:16:59.280
find the Windows
365 product page,

00:16:59.280 --> 00:17:00.720
and determine the best offering

00:17:00.720 --> 00:17:02.880
for a 500-person
remote organization

00:17:02.880 --> 00:17:05.310
across North America and LATAM.

00:17:05.310 --> 00:17:07.440
This is something a person
normally would have

00:17:07.440 --> 00:17:10.530
to research manually
across multiple pages.

00:17:10.530 --> 00:17:12.150
Let's see if the
agent can do it.

00:17:12.150 --> 00:17:14.910
The agent starts working,
connects to a Cloud PC,

00:17:14.910 --> 00:17:17.580
and processes the request.

00:17:17.580 --> 00:17:21.180
And there's the answer, a clear
explanation and a reason why,

00:17:21.180 --> 00:17:22.620
based on information
that it gathered

00:17:22.620 --> 00:17:24.390
from the website directly.

00:17:24.390 --> 00:17:26.040
Let's break down what happened.

00:17:26.040 --> 00:17:28.050
First, it opened
Edge, then searched

00:17:28.050 --> 00:17:30.210
for the Windows
365 product page.

00:17:30.210 --> 00:17:32.130
It waited for the page to load,

00:17:32.130 --> 00:17:34.170
navigated to the
relevant sections,

00:17:34.170 --> 00:17:36.540
and moved into the Enterprise
and Pricing views.

00:17:36.540 --> 00:17:39.510
From there it analyzed the
content, compared options,

00:17:39.510 --> 00:17:41.220
and pulled together
the information needed

00:17:41.220 --> 00:17:43.950
to generate a clear,
well-reasoned recommendation,

00:17:43.950 --> 00:17:46.470
tailored to the
scenario I gave it.

00:17:46.470 --> 00:17:49.230
This just scratches the
surface of what's possible

00:17:49.230 --> 00:17:51.360
with Windows 365 for Agents.

00:17:51.360 --> 00:17:55.020
It enables agents to take on
complex, multi-step tasks

00:17:55.020 --> 00:17:58.880
across real applications and
the web, turning what used

00:17:58.880 --> 00:18:03.120
to be manual work into
automated end-to-end outcomes.

00:18:03.120 --> 00:18:04.710
So let's bring it all together.

00:18:04.710 --> 00:18:06.240
Three things to take away.

00:18:06.240 --> 00:18:09.030
One, stay focused
on agent logic.

00:18:09.030 --> 00:18:12.510
Windows 365 for Agent handles
the execution environment

00:18:12.510 --> 00:18:14.250
underneath, so your time goes

00:18:14.250 --> 00:18:16.530
into what makes your
agent valuable.

00:18:16.530 --> 00:18:20.700
Two, extend the reach of your
agent past the API boundary.

00:18:20.700 --> 00:18:23.310
A lot of enterprise work
still happens in that line

00:18:23.310 --> 00:18:25.590
of business application
that falls outside

00:18:25.590 --> 00:18:27.360
of the modernization efforts.

00:18:27.360 --> 00:18:30.000
With a cloud PC, your
agent can operate them

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:31.800
in the current state.

00:18:31.800 --> 00:18:35.430
Three, build on the one
streamlined Microsoft stack,

00:18:35.430 --> 00:18:39.900
from MCP server to Agent
365 to Intune and Entra.

00:18:39.900 --> 00:18:41.460
Build, publish, govern,

00:18:41.460 --> 00:18:45.120
and scale on your tools your
organization already trusts.

00:18:45.120 --> 00:18:47.370
That's Windows 365 for Agents.

00:18:47.370 --> 00:18:48.690
Back to you, Joydeep.

00:18:48.690 --> 00:18:49.410
JOYDEEP MUKHERJEE: All
right, so we looked

00:18:49.410 --> 00:18:51.540
at Windows 365 for Agents.

00:18:51.540 --> 00:18:52.920
That is just one offering.

00:18:52.920 --> 00:18:56.190
Now, let me walk you
through Windows 365,

00:18:56.190 --> 00:18:59.160
a different offering, and
how it helps developers work

00:18:59.160 --> 00:19:02.010
with less friction.

00:19:02.010 --> 00:19:03.870
As you all know, as developers,

00:19:03.870 --> 00:19:05.970
you guys face new
challenges now.

00:19:05.970 --> 00:19:09.990
Fifty percent or more developer
time is spent on maintenance,

00:19:09.990 --> 00:19:13.110
complex configuration, and
operational tasks rather

00:19:13.110 --> 00:19:14.340
than writing new code.

00:19:14.340 --> 00:19:15.510
The story is familiar, right?

00:19:15.510 --> 00:19:17.670
You sit down to write
code, and then you've got

00:19:17.670 --> 00:19:21.450
to manage your environment,
do a bunch of configurations,

00:19:21.450 --> 00:19:23.340
and this takes cycles
away from what you need

00:19:23.340 --> 00:19:25.620
to be doing, coding
and shipping.

00:19:25.620 --> 00:19:29.940
Seventy-five percent or more
developers need secure,

00:19:29.940 --> 00:19:33.390
virtualized environments
during development.

00:19:33.390 --> 00:19:38.220
Forty percent or more of you
work across platforms like Mac

00:19:38.220 --> 00:19:41.310
and Windows and Linux
and other stuff.

00:19:41.310 --> 00:19:44.400
So these are the
exact challenges

00:19:44.400 --> 00:19:48.700
for which we designed
Windows 365.

00:19:51.180 --> 00:19:54.420
Microsoft is uniquely
positioned to bring the PC

00:19:54.420 --> 00:19:55.800
and the cloud together,

00:19:55.800 --> 00:19:59.160
and Windows 365 delivers
those benefits directly

00:19:59.160 --> 00:20:00.600
to modern development.

00:20:00.600 --> 00:20:03.660
It provides a cloud
development environment

00:20:03.660 --> 00:20:07.410
with a ready-to-code Windows
Cloud PC preconfigured

00:20:07.410 --> 00:20:09.780
with GitHub and preferred
developer tools,

00:20:09.780 --> 00:20:11.640
helping teams get
productive faster

00:20:11.640 --> 00:20:14.100
and reduce environment friction.

00:20:14.100 --> 00:20:17.910
Now, just as Office 365 brought
productivity to the cloud,

00:20:17.910 --> 00:20:20.810
Windows 365 brings
Windows to the cloud.

00:20:20.810 --> 00:20:24.200
For you, this means that the
right environment is right there

00:20:24.200 --> 00:20:26.390
at your fingertips
for each task.

00:20:26.390 --> 00:20:29.210
You can assign one or
multiple cloud PCs,

00:20:29.210 --> 00:20:32.330
each set up for specific
workloads, whether for coding,

00:20:32.330 --> 00:20:35.010
testing, or AI development.

00:20:35.010 --> 00:20:38.760
With Windows App, you can access
your cloud PCs from any device

00:20:38.760 --> 00:20:41.280
and switch seamlessly
between your local desktop

00:20:41.280 --> 00:20:46.560
and cloud environment using
familiar gestures and shortcuts.

00:20:46.560 --> 00:20:49.710
Windows 365 streams a
full Windows experience.

00:20:49.710 --> 00:20:50.730
This is very important.

00:20:50.730 --> 00:20:54.180
Apps, data, settings, everything
from the Microsoft Cloud

00:20:54.180 --> 00:20:57.690
to any device, giving you
consistent, secured access

00:20:57.690 --> 00:21:02.220
without being constrained by
local hardware or config issues.

00:21:02.220 --> 00:21:06.180
With Windows 365, you can focus
less on managing environments,

00:21:06.180 --> 00:21:08.400
more on coding and shipping.

00:21:08.400 --> 00:21:12.120
Let me now walk you through
what Windows 365 can do

00:21:12.120 --> 00:21:13.230
for developers.

00:21:13.230 --> 00:21:15.180
Development today isn't
tied to one machine.

00:21:15.180 --> 00:21:17.100
You can switch between
devices all day,

00:21:17.100 --> 00:21:18.900
and every switch
comes with friction.

00:21:18.900 --> 00:21:20.820
So we start on my
personal Mac device,

00:21:20.820 --> 00:21:24.840
but I'm connecting straight
into my development cloud PC

00:21:24.840 --> 00:21:26.130
through the Windows App.

00:21:26.130 --> 00:21:27.210
You see I have two there.

00:21:27.210 --> 00:21:28.410
I'm choosing today to go

00:21:28.410 --> 00:21:31.560
into the 16-core cloud
PC for development.

00:21:31.560 --> 00:21:34.290
You see that it's setting
it up, configuring it,

00:21:34.290 --> 00:21:37.710
and it will shortly
connect to my cloud PC.

00:21:37.710 --> 00:21:41.820
Now, once I'm inside, look
at what is already on there.

00:21:41.820 --> 00:21:43.980
GitHub Copilot,
Visual Studio Code,

00:21:43.980 --> 00:21:46.020
everything is ready
for me to go.

00:21:46.020 --> 00:21:51.570
Now, today, my project also
requires WinGet, Docker,

00:21:51.570 --> 00:21:53.370
Postman, and a bunch
of other stuff.

00:21:53.370 --> 00:21:57.630
And you see there the cloud PC
automatically puts everything

00:21:57.630 --> 00:21:58.260
on there.

00:21:58.260 --> 00:22:00.360
I don't have to spend
my cycles there.

00:22:00.360 --> 00:22:01.560
It's all ready.

00:22:01.560 --> 00:22:07.470
I log in, and then I am
ready to begin coding.

